      <p>
        A <strong>Qibla finder</strong> is an astronomical and navigational tool designed to help Muslims determine the exact direction toward the <strong>Holy Kaaba</strong> in the Sacred Mosque (<em>al-Masjid al-Haram</em>) in Makkah al-Mukarramah, Saudi Arabia (21.422487° N, 39.826206° E). Facing the Qibla (<em>Istiqbal al-Qiblah</em>) is an obligatory prerequisite (<em>shart</em>) for the validity of the five daily Salah prayers (Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha), as ordained in the Holy Quran:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#171717] dark:border-white pl-4 py-2 my-4 bg-[#fafafa] dark:bg-[#121212] text-[#171717] dark:text-[#ededed] italic rounded-r">
        "So turn your face toward the al-Masjid al-Haram. And wherever you [believers] are, turn your faces toward it [in prayer]." — <em>Surah Al-Baqarah (2:144)</em>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Unlike rough estimations or static regional lookups that lump entire districts together, a modern <strong>qibla finder online</strong> must analyze each user's unique latitude and longitude down to sub-kilometer precision. Because the Earth is a spherical ellipsoid, even a small difference in location—such as moving between neighboring towns like Iritty and Kannur, or Manchester and Leeds—results in measurably distinct Qibla azimuth angles and prayer times. <strong>CheckQibla.com</strong> solves this by evaluating your live GPS coordinates without rounding or regional approximations.
      </p>
