        How the Qibla Finder Compass Works: Spherical Great-Circle Trigonometry
      </h3>
      <p>
        Many generic compass apps simply draw a straight 2D line on a flat Mercator projection map, which creates significant navigational distortion over long distances. Our <strong>qibla finder compass</strong> implements the forward azimuth spherical trigonometry formula across the WGS-84 reference ellipsoid:
      </p>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-[#ebebeb] dark:border-[#262626] bg-[#fafafa] dark:bg-[#121212] font-mono text-xs text-[#171717] dark:text-[#ededed] overflow-x-auto">
        <code>qibla_angle = atan2( sin(Δλ), cos(φ1) * tan(φ2) - sin(φ1) * cos(Δλ) )</code>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-[#888888] dark:text-[#737373]">
        Where <code>φ1</code> represents your exact GPS latitude, <code>φ2</code> represents Kaaba latitude (21.422487°), and <code>Δλ</code> represents the longitudinal difference (λ_Kaaba - λ_user).
      </p>
      <p>
        The resulting bearing angle is measured clockwise from True Geographic North (0°). When you hold your device flat, our sensor fusion engine merges hardware magnetometer readings with gyro smoothing filters to point you directly toward Makkah with real-time haptic and acoustic confirmation.
      </p>
    </div>

